WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:08.112
Welcome to this short course on
LangChain for large

2
00:00:08.112 --> 00:00:09.709
language model application
development.

3
00:00:10.430 --> 00:00:13.408
By prompting an LLM
or large language model,

4
00:00:13.408 --> 00:00:16.386
it is now possible
to develop AI applications

5
00:00:16.386 --> 00:00:18.248
much faster than ever before.

6
00:00:18.949 --> 00:00:20.931
But an application
can require prompting

7
00:00:20.931 --> 00:00:24.565
an LLM multiple times and parsing
its output, and so there's

8
00:00:24.565 --> 00:00:27.869
a lot of glue code
that needs to be written.

9
00:00:28.591 --> 00:00:31.396
LangChain, created by Harrison
Chase makes this

10
00:00:31.396 --> 00:00:35.805
development process much easier. I'm
thrilled to have Harrison here, who

11
00:00:35.805 --> 00:00:39.814
had built this short course in
collaboration with DeepLearning.ai to

12
00:00:39.814 --> 00:00:42.620
teach how to use this amazing tool.


13
00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:45.961
Thanks for having me. I'm
really excited to be here. LangChain

14
00:00:45.961 --> 00:00:48.695
started as an open source framework
for building LLM

15
00:00:48.695 --> 00:00:52.036
applications. It came about when
I was talking to a bunch

16
00:00:52.036 --> 00:00:54.770
of folks in the field who
were building more

17
00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:57.504
complex applications and
saw some common abstractions in terms

18
00:00:57.504 --> 00:00:59.326
of how they were being developed.


19
00:00:59.887 --> 00:01:02.969
And we've been really thrilled at
the community adoption of

20
00:01:02.969 --> 00:01:03.894
LangChain so far.

21
00:01:04.395 --> 00:01:06.005
And so look forward
to sharing it with

22
00:01:06.005 --> 00:01:07.615
everyone here and look
forward to seeing what

23
00:01:07.615 --> 00:01:08.420
people build with it.

24
00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:11.960
And in fact, as a sign of LangChain's
momentum, not

25
00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:15.856
only does it have numerous users, but
there are also many

26
00:01:15.856 --> 00:01:19.751
hundreds of contributors to
the open source, and this has been

27
00:01:19.751 --> 00:01:22.583
instrumental for its
rapid rate of development. This

28
00:01:22.583 --> 00:01:26.124
team really ships code and features
at an amazing pace.

29
00:01:26.866 --> 00:01:29.537
So hopefully,
after this short course, you'll

30
00:01:29.537 --> 00:01:32.971
be able to quickly put together
some really cool

31
00:01:32.971 --> 00:01:35.641
applications using LangChain, and
who knows, maybe

32
00:01:35.641 --> 00:01:38.693
you even decide to
contribute back to the

33
00:01:38.693 --> 00:01:40.220
open source LangChain effort.

34
00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:42.875
LangChain is an
open source development

35
00:01:42.875 --> 00:01:45.088
framework for building
LLM applications.

36
00:01:45.629 --> 00:01:49.257
We have two different packages,
a Python one and a JavaScript one.

37
00:01:49.798 --> 00:01:51.947
They're focused on composition
and modularity. So

38
00:01:51.947 --> 00:01:54.403
they have a lot
of individual components that

39
00:01:54.403 --> 00:01:56.859
can be used in
conjunction with each other

40
00:01:56.859 --> 00:02:01.157
or by themselves. And so that's one
of the key value adds. And then

41
00:02:01.157 --> 00:02:05.455
the other key value add is a bunch
of different use cases. So chains

42
00:02:05.455 --> 00:02:07.911
of ways of combining
these modular components into

43
00:02:07.911 --> 00:02:10.675
more end-to-end applications making it
very easy to get

44
00:02:10.675 --> 00:02:13.438
started with those use
cases. In this class, we'll

45
00:02:13.438 --> 00:02:16.815
cover the common components of LangChain. So
we'll talk about models.

46
00:02:16.815 --> 00:02:20.192
We'll talk about prompts, which are how
you get models to

47
00:02:20.192 --> 00:02:21.727
do useful and interesting things.


48
00:02:22.368 --> 00:02:25.095
We'll talk about indexes, which
are ways of ingesting

49
00:02:25.095 --> 00:02:28.125
data so that you
can combine it with models. And

50
00:02:28.125 --> 00:02:31.760
then we'll talk about chains, which are
more end-to-end use cases along

51
00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:36.002
with agents, which are a very
exciting type of end-to-end use case which uses

52
00:02:36.002 --> 00:02:37.820
the model as a reasoning engine.


